  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 233 AM CDT Sun Sep 13 2026\n\nToday: Shortwave currently dropping south across northern MN will \nmove across the region this morning bringing mostly cloudy skies and \npossibly a brief sprinkle south of HWY 10. High pressure works back \nin this afternoon retuning dry and partly sunny skies to the area. \nWith high pressure overhead tonight expect clear and calm conditions \nwhich will allow temperatures to fall into the low to middle 30s \nacross north-central WI. Widespread frost is possible along and \nnorth of HWY 8, if you have cold sensitive plants plan to bring them \nindoors or cover them. \n\nHeavy Rain Monday into Tuesday: A strong low pressure system, \ncurrernly coming onshore over the PNW will track into the northern \nPlains Monday. Ahead of this system strong isentropic ascent is \nexpected over WI in association with a LLJ. This will likely lead to \na widespread area of moderate rain pushing into the region Monday \nafternoon and continuing into Tuesday. PWATs with this system are \nexpected to climb to near 2\" (around the 99th percentile of \nclimatology) most areas have at a 70-90% chance for at least 1\" of \nrain. Pockets of higher amounts of 2-3\" are possible, mainly across \ncentral WI, with the NBM showing a 30-50% chance for over 2\" west of \nI-39. The severe storm threat with this system appears rather low \nwith a lack in much instability and a strong near surface \ntemperatures inversion.\n\nRest of Next Week: A baroclinic zone is expected set up over or near \nthe forecast area for the middle and end of next week. This will \nkeep a 30-50% chance for showers and occasional thunderstorms \nWednesday through Friday, however, it will likely not rain that \nentire period. Temperatures will hover near to slightly below normal \nnext week depending on where the boundary sets up each day.",
